# Configuration variables, with placeholder values. Copy to .env and fill in.
# Never commit .env — gitleaks blocks it as a pre-commit hook and again in CI.
#
# Everything here is read by the BUILD. The app itself reads no environment
# variable, and that is the point rather than an omission: the MVP declares no
# network permission and stores everything in local SQLite (architecture §1), so
# there is no endpoint, no account, no API key and no telemetry sink to
# configure. A variable that needed to live here to make the app talk to
# something would be a change to the privacy claim in README and PRD §5, not a
# config chore.
#
# Release signing, per architecture §9. AndroidApplicationConventionPlugin reads
# these four through Gradle's provider API, and an unset one leaves the release
# build unsigned instead of failing configuration — which is how CI builds
# without a key at all. `make release` is the only target that needs them and
# checks before it starts.
#
# Nothing reads the .env file itself: the build reads the environment, so export
# them first.
#
#   set -a; . ./.env; set +a
#   make release
#
# The `./` is not a typo: `.` looks a bare name up on $PATH, so `. .env` fails
# in zsh even from the directory holding it. bash falls back to the working
# directory; zsh does not.
#
# The quotes around the placeholders below are not decoration either. `<` and
# `>` are redirection operators, so an unquoted `<placeholder>` is a syntax
# error and sourcing this file aborts at that line — in both shells. Worse, `.`
# runs line by line, so a half-edited file exports the lines above the error and
# nothing below it, which looks exactly like a variable that was never named.
# Keep the quotes, or replace the whole value.
#
# The keystore never enters git. `*.jks` and `*.keystore` are gitignored, which
# is what stands in for gitleaks here — it does not read binaries.
# docs/running.md §6 holds the recipe that makes one and what to do with it
# afterwards. A PKCS12 keystore, which is what keytool writes by default,
# cannot carry a key password that differs from the store's, so those two hold
# the same value unless the keystore is a JKS one.

GAWI_KEYSTORE_PATH="<absolute-path-to-release-keystore>"
GAWI_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD="<keystore-password>"
GAWI_KEY_ALIAS="<key-alias>"
GAWI_KEY_PASSWORD="<same-as-the-keystore-password-on-a-pkcs12-store>"
